}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n1+0+x+x+(-9)+3=2x-5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516874 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186516972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516874 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02df79de-1a4d-5335-b5b6-f27a07496e01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516874 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186284521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186516896 #edb/ref 1058 #e